# ARC-AGI-3 Game Agent — campaign playbook

Read this before building anything for the Kaggle ARC Prize 2026 (ARC-AGI-3) competition.
Companion playbook: `~/.agents/skills/competition-agent-loop/SKILL.md` (campaign discipline —
kill gates, ledgers, seed rules, submission economy — applies verbatim; this file adds the
game-agent specifics). Facts below verified 2026-09-07 against the sources in §6.

## 1. The competition + scoring rules

- **Task:** agents play unseen 2D puzzle games through a generic harness. Observations are
  text renderings of 64x64 grids (ASCII), no rules or goals given — the agent gets the action
  list and must infer everything by interacting. Loop shape: `observe → choose_action →
  observation ... until level done` (`is_done`). 25 game environments; the public set is
  183 levels (playable at arcprize.org/tasks).
- **Scoring = RHAE (Relative Human Action Efficiency).** Human baselines were set by
  in-person testing per game. 100% = beat every game while using no more actions than the
  human baseline. Per level you only get credit if you WIN it, and the efficiency term is
  capped, so wasted actions burn score but extra efficiency doesn't over-reward. Average
  human tester scores ~48% (OpenAI's estimate from official logs). Exact per-game formula:
  confirm in docs.arcprize.org before building the local scorer.
- **Money ($850K total):** $700K Grand Prize for the FIRST agent to score 100% (carries over
  if unclaimed); $75K Top Score Award (guaranteed, 1st=$40K); $75K milestone prizes
  ($25K/$10K/$2.5K each) that REQUIRE open-sourcing. Milestone #1 (June 30 2026) was won by
  Tufa Labs. **Milestone #2 closes Sept 30 2026** — open-source deadline, three weeks out
  from this writing.
- **Eval constraints:** submissions via Kaggle, **no internet during evaluation**, hardware
  limits announced at launch, all code/methods must be open-sourced for prizes. Local
  public-game scores are a KNOWN-unreliable predictor of leaderboard rank (3rd-place winner's
  explicit warning) — tune on public games, gate on held-out behavior diversity, not just
  local RHAE.

## 2. Key facts from top approaches (what actually worked)

- **Tufa Labs "The Duck" (Milestone #1 winner)** — Qwen 3.6 27B FP8 run locally, the only
  winner using agent-writes-code. Treats each game as an interactive coding problem: game
  state becomes Python variables, the model loops reason → write helper functions → execute
  in a live REPL → emit actions → read observation. Perception: rendered image + raw ASCII
  grid + a segmentation tool for zooming into regions; the agent picks per moment. Context
  management: "infinite play via eviction" — continuously pop the oldest messages, keep
  system prompt + recent history. Philosophy: keep the harness lightweight/generic and let
  the model drive; **hand-crafted tools actually hurt**; gains came from multimodality and
  better base models. Lineage: successor to "Stochastic Goose" (ARC-AGI-3 preview winner).
  Write-up: Kaggle discussion 717133.
- **OpenAI (blog 2026-09-03): two settings tripled scores.** GPT-5.6 Sol on the official
  harness: 13.3% on public set; with (a) **retained reasoning** (private reasoning carried
  across turns instead of discarded after every action) and (b) **compaction** (summarize the
  conversation when too long) instead of the official harness's rolling truncation at 175K
  chars: **38.3% — ~3x score with ~6x fewer output tokens**. Two failure modes of the
  official harness: reasoning discarded each action (model re-learns the game every turn) and
  old actions falling out of a truncation window (loses memory of what it did). Lesson for
  ANY model+handroll harness: persist a reasoning/scratchpad channel across turns and compact
  (summarize) rather than hard-truncate.
- **2nd place "Reki":** vision-LLM-as-policy — recent frames rendered as labeled images into
  Gemma-4-31B locally, one JSON object per step (what changed, short plan, 1-4 actions);
  reflection memory refreshed ~every 10 steps; CPU-only numpy click heuristic favoring small,
  rare-colored, button-like shapes; "dead-signature" mechanism stops clicking object types
  that never change anything. Simple beats complex.
- **3rd place "forge":** same look-at-board/return-JSON with Gemma-4-31B, wrapped in a
  candidate-generator + arbiter framework — its best run **turned the machinery off**.
- **NVIDIA AVO (arXiv 2603.24517):** 100% RHAE on the PUBLIC set (all 183 levels) in 6,624
  actions (~36 actions/level — the efficiency bar) with Claude Opus 5, using persistent
  memory + execution loop + stagnation-supervisor. Public set only — withheld sets unsolved.
  Not a Kaggle submission, but it defines the action-efficiency frontier and proves the
  observation format is sufficient.
- **Mostik AI** claims an undisclosed model topping the ARC-AGI-3 leaderboard —
  self-reported, unverifiable, ignore for design decisions (`research/topics/mostik_latent_bridge.md`).

**Synthesis:** you do NOT need RL or world models to place. You need (1) a small capable LLM
in a tight loop, (2) game-as-programming-task framing with a REPL, (3) aggressive context
discipline (eviction or compaction + a persistent scratchpad), (4) simple exploration
heuristics (systematic sweep, dead-signature stop, rare-color clicks), (5) action frugality.
Bespoke tooling beyond that has negative expected value at every placing so far.

