Net: model-serving and campaign machinery are strong; every MISSING item is game-specific
glue (connector, REPL, perception, scorer, bundle) — all small, all buildable in stages.

## 4. Staged replication plan (each stage gated, competition-agent-loop rules apply)

- **Stage 0 — Setup + scorer (day 1).** Read Kaggle overview + docs.arcprize.org; install
  the SDK; run the 25 public games locally; write the replay logger and a local RHAE
  approximator (needs per-level human action baselines from docs/logs). Confirm: Kaggle GPU
  type, whether model weights attach as datasets, per-day submission limits, action budget
  per game. Gate: local scorer reproduces a known trajectory's actions/level.
- **Stage 1 — Baseline JSON-action agent (1-2 days).** Local connector + smallest viable
  policy: observation (ASCII grid) → small local model → JSON {what changed, plan, 1-4
  actions}. Exploration heuristics baked in: systematic ACTION sweep on new levels,
  dead-signature stop-list, rare-color click heuristic (Reki's, ~free on CPU). Local model
  via mlx-lm/llama.cpp on M5 (Qwen3-8B-class to start; test Qwen3-30B-A3B-class MoE — low
  active params may make it viable in 16GB). Gate: wins >0 public levels within the action
  budget; log every trajectory (journal.jsonl).
- **Stage 2 — REPL harness (Duck replication, ~1 week).** Game state → Python variables;
  model writes helper functions and executes in a sandboxed REPL (subprocess + restricted
  globals + timeout); emits 1-4 committed actions per turn. Perception: ASCII + optional
  rendered image + flood-fill segmentation into clickable regions. Gate: beats Stage 1 on
  local RHAE over ALL public games (n=all — no sampling shortcuts), ablated (law 6).
- **Stage 3 — Context management (~3-4 days).** Per-game memory file re-injected compactly
  (learned rules, dead signatures, level map) + persistent scratchpad across turns
  (poor-man's retained reasoning). A/B: Duck-style pure eviction vs summarize-and-compact —
  OpenAI's evidence says compaction + retained reasoning wins (3x score, 6x fewer tokens);
  Duck's evidence says eviction suffices at 27B. Measure, don't assume. Gate: held-out
  games (never-seen public levels replayed fresh), not the tuned-on set.
- **Stage 4 — Action-efficiency tuning (ongoing).** RHAE rewards frugality: plan multi-step
  in the REPL and simulate before committing actions; batch moves; only "spend" actions when
  information gain or progress is expected. Target the AVO bar (~36 actions/level). Every
  harness change gets a held-out gate (HarnessDev: only ~53% of harness tweaks generalize).
- **Stage 5 — Stretch.** (a) AVO/OpenEvolve-style evolution of prompts/heuristics on local
  replays (`pip install openevolve`); (b) KD of a bigger teacher policy into a servable
  student using the verified hybrid recipe if Kaggle compute caps the model size; (c) if
  Kaggle GPU allows, swap in the largest Qwen that fits and re-gate.

## 5. Hard constraints — M5 Mac (16GB) + Kaggle

- **16GB unified memory is the binding constraint.** Verified: Bonsai 27B ternary 2-bit via
  MLX = 7.86GB peak, 5.3 tok/s; 8B 2-bit = 16 tok/s; llama.cpp Metal OOM'd on the same 27B
  (macOS wires well under 16GB to GPU). 27B-in-GPU + anything GPU-heavy cannot coexist —
  serialize GPU workloads. Watch `kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory`.
- **MLX is Apple-only.** Whatever runs on Kaggle's NVIDIA GPUs must be llama.cpp (CUDA build)
  or transformers/vLLM — pick model formats that exist in BOTH worlds (GGUF for Kaggle,
  MLX 4-bit for local dev of the same model). Test the Kaggle-side path early (Stage 1).
- **No docker locally.** Embedded/local servers only (pattern: hindsight-embed, mlx-lm CLI,
  llama-server). `uv` for all envs: `uv run --with mlx-lm ...`.
- **llama.cpp custom-format trap:** Bonsai's ternary formats lack Metal kernels in stock brew
  llama.cpp → silent CPU fallback (11+ min stall, zero tokens). On Mac use MLX builds; GGUF
  only for the CUDA side.
- **No internet at Kaggle eval** → the agent must be fully offline: bundled weights as a
  Kaggle dataset, no API calls, deterministic startup. Rehearse the exact submission bundle
  locally with networking disabled before submitting.
- **Local-vs-leaderboard gap is documented** (3rd place winner) — don't over-fit to public
  games; keep a falsified-list and per-change gates like any other campaign.

